WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.552 --> 00:00:08.760
SOPHIA YANG: Hello, everyone.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.870
Thank you so much
for being here today.

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:13.930
We are very excited to
see all of you here.

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:15.890
We are also very eager
to share with you

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:20.480
our perspectives about how to
better manage your VM instance.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.450
My name is Sophia Yang, product
manager from the Compute Engine

00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:24.270
team.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:27.280
Joining me today are two of
my coworkers, [? Karen ?]

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:30.900
and [INAUDIBLE],, who will help
with some very awesome demos.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.060
So let's get started.

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:37.780
When talking about running
workload on Compute Engine,

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:41.650
VM instances are really at the
heart and center of everything.

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:43.990
We want to make sure you
have the right instance

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.990
to run your job, but also,
you don't want to pay more.

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:48.670
And that's exactly
the first section

00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:50.770
we're going to be
talking about, how

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:52.750
to tune a VM instance
so that you have

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:56.110
the best in price performance.

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:58.580
Next, we're talking
about scaling.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:01.640
Most workloads are now done
with a single VM instance.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.489
How can you scale
up and down quickly?

00:01:04.489 --> 00:01:06.590
That is the second
section we'll be covering,

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:09.570
both the tools and the demo
to show you the performance

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.140
that we can achieve.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:13.970
Third, backing up.

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:15.920
No critical
workloads can be done

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.340
without a good backup recovery
story, and in that section,

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:20.960
we have brand-new
features to share

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:25.230
with you to help you better
back up your VM instance.

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:28.440
And last but not least, secure.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.930
How can you make sure
you can manage the access

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:33.830
control to your VM
instance so that your data,

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:36.450
your environment,
is all protected?

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:39.120
And at Google,
security and privacy

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:40.694
is always our first priority.

00:01:40.694 --> 00:01:42.360
And there, we're going
to share with you

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.745
some tips and new
features about how

00:01:44.745 --> 00:01:49.710
to better control the security
and to secure a VM instance.

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:53.130
First, let's talk about tuning.

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:56.130
Picking the right VM instance.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:58.740
So if you are going to ask
me, what is the right VM

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.290
instance for me,
then I can't tell you

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.360
that, because I have
to ask you back,

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.000
what does your
workload look like?

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.850
The right answer is, the
VM instance size always

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:10.289
depends on workload.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:12.270
So let's first look at
the popular workload

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.280
running today in cloud.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.060
Here is just a list of them,
and the list goes on on and on.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.910
Basically, at
Google, we understand

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:23.700
customers come with a
diverse set of workloads.

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:26.340
They all have a diverse
set of requirements.

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:29.580
And we are providing a
wider range of selection

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:31.620
of machine types
in the VM instance

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.590
and features to make sure we
can meet all your workload

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:36.620
requirements.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:40.080
Here is just a overview
of the general categories

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.750
of the machine types we
are offering at Google.

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:47.970
We have general purpose, memory
optimized, and shared cores.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:51.390
Within each family, we also
allow great flexibility

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:54.960
so that you don't have to think
too much on the constraints

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:56.790
in terms of what you
should do in order

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:59.280
to utilize certain features.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:00.820
What do I mean by that?

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:03.390
For example, let's dive into
the general purpose machine

00:03:03.390 --> 00:03:07.440
types, which is our most
popular machine type.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.590
Within a general purpose
pool, we have three families--

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:14.170
standard, high
memory and high CPU.

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:17.000
They are really designed to
be suitable for a majority

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.526
of your workloads.

00:03:18.526 --> 00:03:19.900
So if you have
the web server you

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:23.250
are trying to host, the CI/CD
workload you are trying to run,

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:25.350
chances are you
can find the best

00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:29.030
suited machine within this
general purpose machine type.

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:33.520
They're highly available
across all regions and zones,

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:35.530
and just remember, I
mentioned that they

00:03:35.530 --> 00:03:37.360
are very flexible to use.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:41.840
For example, you can attach
local SSDs and the GPUs

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:43.300
to all of them.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.550
And like at other
cloud providers,

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:47.330
you have to think
about what size

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:49.720
and what generations do
I have to use in order

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:51.890
to, for example, use GPUs.

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:53.950
At Google, general
purpose machine types,

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:57.850
you can attach GPUs and
local SSDs to all of them.

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:02.020
Better than that, we also
allowed a wider range of core

00:04:02.020 --> 00:04:03.250
to GPU ratios.

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:05.530
For example, at
Google, if you only

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:08.765
need a 1 core VM to
do a job, but you

00:04:08.765 --> 00:04:12.340
would like to have 4 GPUs to
have better parallel computing,

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.310
you can do that combination.

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:16.450
But not unlike other
cloud providers,

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:20.050
you have to have a much bigger
VM in order to have 4 GPUs.

00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:25.950
Within the predefined
families, all the machines

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:30.330
come with 1, 2 4, all
the way up to 96 cores.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.160
So the next very
natural question

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:35.550
will be, say I picked
the 8 core machine.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.290
How can I make
sure I'm maximizing

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:38.280
all the compute power?

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:40.200
How can I make
sure I'm utilizing

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.240
all the money I'm spending?

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.430
And that is an
excellent question.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:49.560
In fact, RightScale recently
did a study showing the estimate

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:53.110
about 35% of cloud
spending is all wasted.

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:55.110
Why waste it?

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:58.530
Just as an example, I mentioned
you have a 8 core machine,

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:00.120
right, but your
workload actually

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:04.770
probably only needs 6 cores,
so there's 2 cores wasted.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:08.460
Another more extreme example,
what if your workload needs,

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:10.310
say, 18 cores.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:13.320
So a 16 core machine
wouldn't be enough,

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.270
while the next in line
if you need to jump to

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:16.740
will be 32 cores.

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:20.920
So that will be almost double
the compute power you need.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.000
So what are your options there?

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.580
You can, one, pay
double the money

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:28.380
so that you have the
maximum performance,

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.780
or you rewrite your
code, the architecture,

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:32.340
so that, you know,
your workload run

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:35.170
could fit in better
in the 16 cores.

00:05:35.170 --> 00:05:37.490
None of them ideal
options, right?

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:40.260
And in fact, all
the cloud providers

00:05:40.260 --> 00:05:43.260
know that not all the
predefined machine sizes

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.960
can fit all the workloads best.

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:48.590
But why haven't they
done anything yet?

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:51.290
The truth is, the cores
that you are now using,

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:53.210
that's great news for
the cloud provider,

00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:56.110
because they can then sell
that to other people, right?

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:58.350
But at Google, it's different.

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:00.080
We want you to get
exactly what you

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:03.170
need without paying any
more, and that is why

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:06.140
we offer custom machine types.

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:08.570
As the name suggests,
you can customize

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:11.330
a number of cores
and memories exactly

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:14.950
as you want with all the
flexibility you want.

00:06:14.950 --> 00:06:19.430
With custom machine types, you
can even achieve higher memory

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:22.820
to CPU ratio compared to
general purpose machines.

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:25.490
This is a great use case
if you have certain, say,

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:28.850
licensing requirements, if
your OIs or [? application ?]

00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:31.890
providers, say, are charging
you based on the number of cores

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:32.390
you have.

00:06:32.390 --> 00:06:34.670
So you want to kind of
be conservative there,

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:36.650
but you also want a
large amount of memory

00:06:36.650 --> 00:06:38.630
to maximize your
workload performance.

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:40.850
And you can do that
with extended memory

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:44.410
within the custom
machine type family.

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:47.380
To get started with custom
machine types, it's very easy.

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:50.050
Just define the number
of cores and memories

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:54.450
you need as that example
shows on the screen.

00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:56.280
In this example,
you create a new VM

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:57.990
with a custom machine type.

00:06:57.990 --> 00:07:01.200
You can also updating
existing machines

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:06.810
to a newer VM shape, which
is based off in a VM first.

00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:09.450
As mentioned, general
purpose machine types

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:10.905
is the most popular
machine types

00:07:10.905 --> 00:07:14.220
at Google, plus the
custom machine type.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:16.740
They should be able to
achieve the performance you

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:19.530
want with most of your
workload requirements.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:22.470
And in fact, more than
99% of our customers

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:24.180
today are running on
these custom machine

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:26.400
types plus the general
purpose machine types.

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:32.280
And next, let's see
some other options

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:35.580
and features you might utilize
to maximize your workload

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:37.900
performance.

00:07:37.900 --> 00:07:41.050
The next one I want to touch
about is the preemptible VMs.

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:45.590
What they are, they're really
just short-lived, low-cost VMs.

00:07:45.590 --> 00:07:47.260
How short-lived?

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:49.750
They can only run
up to 24 hours.

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:53.990
And that is if not Google
preempted you before that.

00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:58.130
Google might preempt your VMs
at any time with 30 seconds'

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:00.455
notification, so
your workload better

00:08:00.455 --> 00:08:04.070
be pretty robust so that you can
handle that kind of preemption.

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:05.970
And what is the plus side?

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:07.190
The price.

00:08:07.190 --> 00:08:10.190
You can save up to 80%
compared to the listing

00:08:10.190 --> 00:08:13.020
price for regular VMs.

00:08:13.020 --> 00:08:15.300
Another very powerful
thing to realize,

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:18.060
you can make any machine
types available at Google

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:19.890
as preemptible VMs.

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:23.670
You can just change a setting or
property within a VM instance,

00:08:23.670 --> 00:08:27.940
make it a preemptible,
and there you have it.

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:30.210
They're really
suited for, say you

00:08:30.210 --> 00:08:33.460
have a fault-tolerant workload
as a batch job, or media

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:38.309
rendering, genomic kind of
jobs or short-lived jobs.

00:08:38.309 --> 00:08:39.690
They are not very
suitable if you

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:41.523
have a job that you
have a certain deadline,

00:08:41.523 --> 00:08:45.104
say, in the next hour, you
want to run a business report,

00:08:45.104 --> 00:08:46.895
we don't recommend you
run preemptible VMs.

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:53.690
Next, memory optimized VMs.

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:57.350
We know a lot of enterprises,
probably many of you sit here,

00:08:57.350 --> 00:09:00.230
you're in the process of
migrating your workload

00:09:00.230 --> 00:09:03.290
from your data center to cloud.

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:05.990
We realize one of the
biggest challenges

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:08.450
to move those large
in-memory databases,

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:13.070
like SAP HANA, a lot of times
because they couldn't find

00:09:13.070 --> 00:09:16.010
a suitable hardware on cloud
that can run your big memory

00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:18.950
machines, customers
are forced to set up

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:20.830
this hybrid environment,
because they already

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:24.230
moved their applications,
web service onto the cloud.

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:27.400
Yet, they have to leave their
memory optimized VM behind

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:31.524
because they just couldn't
find the right thing in cloud.

00:09:31.524 --> 00:09:33.190
Well, if you are one
of those customers,

00:09:33.190 --> 00:09:35.570
you'll be happy to find
out that Google offers

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:39.170
the memory optimized VMs,
which can provide you up

00:09:39.170 --> 00:09:42.290
to 3.8 terabyte memories.

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:44.300
That is a lot of memories.

00:09:44.300 --> 00:09:47.460
Within the memory optimized
VMs we have four machine types,

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:48.380
as you can see.

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:54.530
And you can achieve as high
as 24 gigabytes per CPO ratio.

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:57.212
And you might be thinking, oh,
these must be very expensive.

00:09:57.212 --> 00:09:59.420
Well, they probably are more
expensive than the share

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:01.520
cores or one core VM.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:05.690
But if you look at it
a price per gig ratio,

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:09.140
they are actually the cheapest
among all of machine types.

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:12.210
So they're also
very cost-effective.

00:10:12.210 --> 00:10:13.730
So if you're one
of those customers

00:10:13.730 --> 00:10:16.130
you have a big, you
know, memory database

00:10:16.130 --> 00:10:18.830
like SAP HANA
[INAUDIBLE],, now you never

00:10:18.830 --> 00:10:23.310
have to leave them behind while
move all the workload on cloud.

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:26.420
So go ahead and try those
memory optimized VMs.

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:28.550
And if you missed the
news from last week,

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:30.590
we're happy to remind
you that we just

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:32.990
made these memory
optimized VMs to be

00:10:32.990 --> 00:10:34.430
generally available for you.

00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:40.700
Well, after talking about
memory intensive workload,

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:44.450
let's talk about compute
intensive workload.

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:48.090
Last February, also
at Next, Google

00:10:48.090 --> 00:10:50.550
announced the
general availability

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:54.390
of Skylake CPUs, which made
us the first ever cloud

00:10:54.390 --> 00:10:59.460
provider support the
latest Intel processor.

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:04.050
Skylake benchmark shows if we
enable the advanced instruction

00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:09.420
ABX 512, it can double the
floating point calculation

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:10.930
compared to the
previous generation.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:14.490
So it's powerful if you have
a high compute intensive

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:15.480
workload.

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:20.160
They are perfect for scientific
modeling, 3D rendering.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:25.080
And in order to utilize
the Skylake CPU,

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:28.710
you can utilize our mean CPU
platform selector function

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:30.260
within a VM instance.

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:31.860
As the example
shows, you can just

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:35.850
define, say, mean CPU platform
equals to Intel Skylake.

00:11:35.850 --> 00:11:37.750
Then Google will make
sure your VM will land

00:11:37.750 --> 00:11:40.260
on a hardware that has Skylake.

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:43.008
Or even later generations
when they are available.

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:50.580
All right, since we were talking
about use cases of customers,

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:53.280
we have seen in other
common cases is that--

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.890
especially from certain
verticals or industries

00:11:55.890 --> 00:11:58.770
like finance, health
care, the customers

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:01.440
have regulations to
meet in terms of where

00:12:01.440 --> 00:12:03.607
they can place the VM instance.

00:12:03.607 --> 00:12:05.190
And if you are one
of those customers,

00:12:05.190 --> 00:12:07.270
you'll be happy
to find out Google

00:12:07.270 --> 00:12:11.130
offers sole tenant nodes, which
are physical servers dedicated

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:13.900
solely for you to use.

00:12:13.900 --> 00:12:16.380
So different from
regular VMs where

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:19.560
you have to share the physical
host with other customers.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:23.160
You can achieve
performance isolation

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:27.180
as you have this whole server
dedicated just for you.

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:29.100
To get started,
it's very simple.

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:33.520
Just create a sole tenant
group using the template.

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:35.130
And then you can
schedule VM instances

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:37.590
onto those sole tenant groups.

00:12:37.590 --> 00:12:40.380
In terms of where the
VM will be placed,

00:12:40.380 --> 00:12:43.740
we have great flexibility
offered there too.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:47.520
So you can obviously
directly explicitly tell us

00:12:47.520 --> 00:12:49.680
where the group
you want to target.

00:12:49.680 --> 00:12:52.170
Or you can use labels
and label selectors.

00:12:52.170 --> 00:12:53.700
So you know to utilize that.

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:56.190
You will first define
labels for those group.

00:12:56.190 --> 00:12:58.264
And you can have multiple
labels for each group.

00:12:58.264 --> 00:12:59.930
And then when you're
scheduling the VMs,

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:02.310
you are targeting those labels.

