00:27:36.150 --> 00:27:39.740
So next, let's talk
about backing up.

00:27:39.740 --> 00:27:42.770
As mentioned, no matter
you have this database,

00:27:42.770 --> 00:27:44.630
all share the file
system, whatever

00:27:44.630 --> 00:27:48.050
the important workload, you want
to have a great backup story,

00:27:48.050 --> 00:27:49.940
to periodically back up
the data to make sure

00:27:49.940 --> 00:27:52.859
you have a great
checkpoint to go back.

00:27:52.859 --> 00:27:54.650
So in this section,
we're going to tell you

00:27:54.650 --> 00:27:58.770
two tools you can use to better
back up your VM instance.

00:27:58.770 --> 00:28:01.040
The first one is to use
Instance Template, which

00:28:01.040 --> 00:28:03.420
is similar as what
we attached earlier.

00:28:03.420 --> 00:28:06.910
We mentioned about using
template create multiple VMs.

00:28:06.910 --> 00:28:08.720
And in this case,
you can also use

00:28:08.720 --> 00:28:11.000
the other, which is
creating instant template

00:28:11.000 --> 00:28:12.740
from the existing VM.

00:28:12.740 --> 00:28:15.350
Why this is useful, because
we have heard of many,

00:28:15.350 --> 00:28:17.510
the use cases especially,
so a developer

00:28:17.510 --> 00:28:19.340
trying to prototype
something starting off

00:28:19.340 --> 00:28:20.870
from a new project.

00:28:20.870 --> 00:28:23.360
They often start with
a VM and then tweak it

00:28:23.360 --> 00:28:24.610
to exactly where they want it.

00:28:24.610 --> 00:28:25.760
Maybe add the disk.

00:28:25.760 --> 00:28:28.070
You know, maybe change
the network, et cetera.

00:28:28.070 --> 00:28:29.720
So once they're are
done to tweak it

00:28:29.720 --> 00:28:32.330
to exact the golden state,
they might not actually

00:28:32.330 --> 00:28:34.575
need a VM, you know,
because of prototyping,

00:28:34.575 --> 00:28:36.950
but they want to make sure
they save the configuration so

00:28:36.950 --> 00:28:40.510
that they can go back
to recreate that.

00:28:40.510 --> 00:28:42.320
And if you have that
kind of requirement,

00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:45.500
you can generate a instant
template from the VM.

00:28:45.500 --> 00:28:47.120
To use the instant
template is almost

00:28:47.120 --> 00:28:49.970
like the configuration
of the time

00:28:49.970 --> 00:28:51.900
machine of the
configuration of your VM,

00:28:51.900 --> 00:28:55.550
so they can go back at any time,
without paying any extra money,

00:28:55.550 --> 00:28:58.340
as the VM is already deleted.

00:28:58.340 --> 00:29:00.500
To get started is also simple.

00:29:00.500 --> 00:29:02.360
As the example shows
on G Cloud, you

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:04.790
can just simply provide
a source instance

00:29:04.790 --> 00:29:07.359
while you're creating
the instant template.

00:29:07.359 --> 00:29:08.900
So this is another
great tool to know

00:29:08.900 --> 00:29:12.080
how to save your configuration
of your VM instance.

00:29:12.080 --> 00:29:15.490
And next, obviously, we were
talking about backing up,

00:29:15.490 --> 00:29:17.240
but the most important
thing is to back up

00:29:17.240 --> 00:29:19.830
your data on the disk.

00:29:19.830 --> 00:29:21.470
You probably know
about a snapshot.

00:29:21.470 --> 00:29:23.928
We already provide that feature
and that's a great feature.

00:29:23.928 --> 00:29:26.720
A very high-performance
and space-efficient

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:29.360
offering that Google
already offered.

00:29:29.360 --> 00:29:32.930
But you might want to ask, how
can I periodically generating

00:29:32.930 --> 00:29:34.700
snapshots?

00:29:34.700 --> 00:29:38.564
Well, without any help from
Google, you can use a cron job,

00:29:38.564 --> 00:29:39.980
but customers have
been telling us

00:29:39.980 --> 00:29:43.730
it's a pain to manage,
especially without including

00:29:43.730 --> 00:29:45.807
deletion or how to manage that.

00:29:45.807 --> 00:29:47.390
And if you are one
of those customers,

00:29:47.390 --> 00:29:49.640
you'll be really
happy to find out

00:29:49.640 --> 00:29:54.180
Google now will offer you to
schedule the snapshot feature.

00:29:54.180 --> 00:29:58.580
What this does is exactly
meant to replace your cron job.

00:29:58.580 --> 00:30:02.240
What a scheduled snapshot
support is will support

00:30:02.240 --> 00:30:04.880
the frequency of creation
snapshots would be hour-, day-,

00:30:04.880 --> 00:30:08.840
and week-based, and will
also support retention policy

00:30:08.840 --> 00:30:11.810
as the number of days you
want to keep the snapshot.

00:30:11.810 --> 00:30:13.900
So you can simply create
a schedule looks like,

00:30:13.900 --> 00:30:19.250
create me a daily snapshot
and then delete after 14 days.

00:30:19.250 --> 00:30:20.900
Very flexible to use.

00:30:20.900 --> 00:30:24.376
You can create a schedule and
then attach to multiple disk

00:30:24.376 --> 00:30:26.000
so that you don't
have to do that again

00:30:26.000 --> 00:30:28.550
and again on every disk.

00:30:28.550 --> 00:30:31.095
One thing to call out--

00:30:31.095 --> 00:30:33.505
the snapshots created the
same as regular snapshots.

00:30:33.505 --> 00:30:35.140
They are incremental snapshots.

00:30:35.140 --> 00:30:37.520
Sometimes people call
differential snapshots.

00:30:37.520 --> 00:30:39.890
What this does is it
only takes a snapshot

00:30:39.890 --> 00:30:43.470
of the delta of the change
between the end time and plus

00:30:43.470 --> 00:30:43.970
one.

00:30:43.970 --> 00:30:45.510
So you have a very
minimal change

00:30:45.510 --> 00:30:47.987
or if no change between the
two times, your snapshot set

00:30:47.987 --> 00:30:49.070
is going to be very small.

00:30:49.070 --> 00:30:51.750
So you are not paying
the whole disk size.

00:30:51.750 --> 00:30:55.460
So very space-efficient,
high-performance,

00:30:55.460 --> 00:30:58.630
and also price-efficient.

00:30:58.630 --> 00:31:01.179
And you will be very
excited to find out

00:31:01.179 --> 00:31:03.470
the this feature going to be
available for you for beta

00:31:03.470 --> 00:31:04.950
very soon.

