00:31:20.630 --> 00:31:24.600
As Sophia mentioned, now taking
periodic backup of your disk

00:31:24.600 --> 00:31:26.710
has been made really easy.

00:31:26.710 --> 00:31:30.490
All you need to do are
two steps using G Cloud.

00:31:30.490 --> 00:31:34.300
First is to create a schedule
in which you specify a start

00:31:34.300 --> 00:31:38.640
time that tells us the time
of the day at which you

00:31:38.640 --> 00:31:42.550
want the snapshots of
the disks to be taken.

00:31:42.550 --> 00:31:45.760
And then specify the
frequency for those snapshots.

00:31:45.760 --> 00:31:48.750
And as Sophia mentioned,
it could be hourly.

00:31:48.750 --> 00:31:51.550
Like, every hour,
every 12 hours.

00:31:51.550 --> 00:31:53.365
It could be daily,
or it could be

00:31:53.365 --> 00:31:55.470
just certain days of the week.

00:31:55.470 --> 00:31:58.410
And once you create
the schedule,

00:31:58.410 --> 00:32:04.530
we also need to specify some
retention with max retention

00:32:04.530 --> 00:32:09.240
days so that we know how long
to keep your snapshots for.

00:32:09.240 --> 00:32:12.840
And after those many days, we
automatically clean them off

00:32:12.840 --> 00:32:15.020
for you.

00:32:15.020 --> 00:32:17.270
Once the schedule
is created, you

00:32:17.270 --> 00:32:22.370
can add it to one on multiple
disks within the region.

00:32:22.370 --> 00:32:27.020
And it can be done
on an existing disk

00:32:27.020 --> 00:32:32.030
or it can be done while you
are creating your disk itself.

00:32:32.030 --> 00:32:38.100
So since these snapshots are
created as per the schedule,

00:32:38.100 --> 00:32:42.150
so I can't demo this thing
