00:35:23.220 --> 00:35:26.100
So last point I want to talk
about involves security.

00:35:26.100 --> 00:35:28.870
How is your secured
access to a VM instance?

00:35:28.870 --> 00:35:30.370
How can you protect
the environment?

00:35:30.370 --> 00:35:32.203
So there are three
things that we would like

00:35:32.203 --> 00:35:33.760
to introduce in that section.

00:35:33.760 --> 00:35:38.250
The first thing is the famous
Identity and Access Management,

00:35:38.250 --> 00:35:40.530
or so-called IAM.

00:35:40.530 --> 00:35:43.100
For the few people that
don't know what IAM is,

00:35:43.100 --> 00:35:45.390
I will just give you
a quick crash course.

00:35:45.390 --> 00:35:51.537
IAM is about defining who can do
which actions on what resource.

00:35:51.537 --> 00:35:52.620
So that's abstract, right?

00:35:52.620 --> 00:35:53.830
Let's just have an example.

00:35:53.830 --> 00:35:57.380
So you could assign me,
Sophia, the instance admin role

00:35:57.380 --> 00:35:59.160
onto your test project.

00:35:59.160 --> 00:36:02.100
That's going to allow me
to manipulate and manage

00:36:02.100 --> 00:36:04.890
your instances in
your test project.

00:36:04.890 --> 00:36:07.720
But you did not give me the same
permission to your production

00:36:07.720 --> 00:36:08.220
project.

00:36:08.220 --> 00:36:11.320
So I wouldn't be able to do
so on the production project.

00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:15.190
So that's kind of fine
control on access control.

00:36:15.190 --> 00:36:20.340
So with the newly released
resource-level IAM feature, now

00:36:20.340 --> 00:36:22.680
you are able to
assign IAM policies

00:36:22.680 --> 00:36:26.140
on individual resources
like VMs, disks.

00:36:26.140 --> 00:36:30.450
Well, this allowing you to
say common use case will be.

00:36:30.450 --> 00:36:33.240
I have one instance I would
like to collaborate with you,

00:36:33.240 --> 00:36:34.240
troubleshoot with you.

00:36:34.240 --> 00:36:36.120
But I don't want to share
everything else in the project

00:36:36.120 --> 00:36:36.640
with you.

00:36:36.640 --> 00:36:39.010
So I don't want to set
a policy on a project.

00:36:39.010 --> 00:36:41.910
Instead, I want to set IAM
policy on the instance.

00:36:41.910 --> 00:36:44.040
And you can achieve
this by simply,

00:36:44.040 --> 00:36:45.930
as the example command
shows on the screen,

00:36:45.930 --> 00:36:48.670
you can set IAM policy
on a particular instance.

00:36:48.670 --> 00:36:51.170
And I will have the
full access to it.

00:36:51.170 --> 00:36:52.680
With that, I would
like to also give

00:36:52.680 --> 00:36:56.920
you guys a quick demo on how
this resource level IAM work.

00:36:56.920 --> 00:36:58.440
And by the way,
this feature is also

00:36:58.440 --> 00:37:01.340
going to be available
for beta very soon.

00:37:01.340 --> 00:37:03.042
Can we switch to
demo real quick?

