And I copy his name.

00:38:03.640 --> 00:38:06.040
And I will go ahead
into my console.

00:38:06.040 --> 00:38:09.170
My view, which I am the
owner of the project.

00:38:09.170 --> 00:38:11.300
I can go ahead set
the permission,

00:38:11.300 --> 00:38:15.640
which is opening the side panel
and adding the member of him

00:38:15.640 --> 00:38:22.150
and giving him the permission
of say, instance admin role.

00:38:22.150 --> 00:38:24.960
And add.

00:38:24.960 --> 00:38:27.810
Simply by doing so, I'm
giving him the permission

00:38:27.810 --> 00:38:31.140
to the particular
instance that was 11.

00:38:31.140 --> 00:38:33.570
So let's go back to his view.

00:38:33.570 --> 00:38:38.470
Let's refresh this console.

00:38:38.470 --> 00:38:41.210
And now you can see this
particular instance 11,

00:38:41.210 --> 00:38:44.200
he has [INAUDIBLE] and
he has the permission

00:38:44.200 --> 00:38:49.070
to stop or suspend
the VM instance.

00:38:49.070 --> 00:38:52.150
So that's the
resource-level IAM policy

00:38:52.150 --> 00:38:54.790
going to be available
for you beta very soon.

00:38:54.790 --> 00:38:56.775
Let's move back to
the presentation.

00:38:59.450 --> 00:39:01.580
And next I want to
mention is about how

00:39:01.580 --> 00:39:04.730
to protect your
operating system.

00:39:04.730 --> 00:39:06.260
How to prevent
people that are not

00:39:06.260 --> 00:39:09.350
authorized to be able to
access into a machine?

00:39:09.350 --> 00:39:11.690
And that is OS
Login is a feature

00:39:11.690 --> 00:39:13.260
you have to learn there.

00:39:13.260 --> 00:39:15.980
So before the OS Login
feature available,

00:39:15.980 --> 00:39:18.680
the world was like, the
only way to log in on VM

00:39:18.680 --> 00:39:21.760
is through setting the
SSH key IAM VM instance

00:39:21.760 --> 00:39:25.400
metadata or the metadata
on the project level.

00:39:25.400 --> 00:39:29.470
The problem is that is, as
soon as the SSH key is set,

00:39:29.470 --> 00:39:32.300
there is no connection
whatsoever with the SSH

00:39:32.300 --> 00:39:34.160
key versus the user.

00:39:34.160 --> 00:39:36.620
So that's making
managing the access

00:39:36.620 --> 00:39:40.299
or revoking keys very
difficult. So this employee

00:39:40.299 --> 00:39:41.840
could have already
left your company,

00:39:41.840 --> 00:39:46.740
but as long as he still knows
the key value [? pair ?] on SSH

00:39:46.740 --> 00:39:48.290
key, he can potentially
still log in

00:39:48.290 --> 00:39:50.000
to your production, environment.

00:39:50.000 --> 00:39:53.130
And that expose a
lot of risk to you.

00:39:53.130 --> 00:39:57.770
With OS Login, we made a strong
connection between the SSH key

00:39:57.770 --> 00:40:00.620
and the user identity,
which IAM control.

00:40:00.620 --> 00:40:04.070
So that user no longer logging
in by just the metadata

00:40:04.070 --> 00:40:06.170
set on the SSH keys.

00:40:06.170 --> 00:40:10.150
Instead, the user will log
in with their own identity.

00:40:10.150 --> 00:40:12.380
With OS Login we have
two roles available.

00:40:12.380 --> 00:40:15.310
IAM roles available
to assign to users.

00:40:15.310 --> 00:40:19.940
OS Login, which is the normal,
non-root access kind of user.

00:40:19.940 --> 00:40:23.090
And OS Admin log in,
obviously, offers root access.

00:40:23.090 --> 00:40:26.390
But no matter which role
the user is assigned to,

00:40:26.390 --> 00:40:27.950
they will never log in as root.

00:40:27.950 --> 00:40:30.520
They always log
in as themselves.

00:40:30.520 --> 00:40:34.830
So that is also making
auditing very easy later on.

00:40:34.830 --> 00:40:37.670
And we also offer Admin
SDK Directory API,

00:40:37.670 --> 00:40:41.990
which allowing the admins
to manage and set those SSH

00:40:41.990 --> 00:40:44.570
keys on behalf of users.

00:40:44.570 --> 00:40:47.720
So OS Login, great feature to
manage your operating system

00:40:47.720 --> 00:40:49.690
access.

00:40:49.690 --> 00:40:51.575
And the very last
feature I would

00:40:51.575 --> 00:40:54.470
like to share with you to help
with better access control,

00:40:54.470 --> 00:40:57.800
is organizational
policy, Org Policy.

00:40:57.800 --> 00:41:02.240
Org Policy is really designed
to work side by side with IAM.

00:41:02.240 --> 00:41:04.850
But the key
difference between IAM

00:41:04.850 --> 00:41:07.690
is Org Policy has the
enforcement, regardless

00:41:07.690 --> 00:41:09.740
this IAM access.

00:41:09.740 --> 00:41:14.040
For example, we have the first
one, No External IP Org Policy.

00:41:14.040 --> 00:41:16.040
Say if you set Org Policy
on to the org level

00:41:16.040 --> 00:41:18.890
and set deny to all
the VM instances that

00:41:18.890 --> 00:41:21.080
can now create its own IP.

00:41:21.080 --> 00:41:24.260
So then even your security
admin, the computer admin,

00:41:24.260 --> 00:41:26.470
who should have the IAM
permission to create

00:41:26.470 --> 00:41:28.970
external IP, will
not be able to do so.

00:41:28.970 --> 00:41:31.190
Again, because the enforcement
is there regardless

00:41:31.190 --> 00:41:32.750
of IAM permission.

00:41:32.750 --> 00:41:36.800
So that enables you to
prevent human error.

00:41:36.800 --> 00:41:40.201
Also, enable you to prevent
certain internal malicious

00:41:40.201 --> 00:41:40.700
user.

00:41:40.700 --> 00:41:44.000
Of course, we hope you
don't have any of those.

00:41:44.000 --> 00:41:46.960
And there are a couple more
Org Policies you can utilize.

00:41:46.960 --> 00:41:49.610
You can set VM
instance, trusted image.

00:41:49.610 --> 00:41:53.720
Make sure you limit what
images developers can use.

00:41:53.720 --> 00:41:56.550
You want to make sure they
only use supported versions.

00:41:56.550 --> 00:41:58.160
Disable serial port access.

00:41:58.160 --> 00:41:59.840
Again, to protect
your OS system so

00:41:59.840 --> 00:42:02.510
that no sensitive information
can get out from the serial

00:42:02.510 --> 00:42:05.200
console.

